      "heading": "Decision trees: a sequence of questions",
      "narration": "The second family reasons by asking questions, and it is the most human-looking model in this course. A decision tree repeatedly splits the data on one feature at one threshold. Is the cap red? Yes goes left, no goes right. Within the left branch, is the stem longer than four centimetres? And so on, until each group at the bottom — each leaf — is nearly pure, containing almost entirely one class. To predict, you walk a new example down the questions and read off the majority class of the leaf it lands in. The whole art is in one question: which split do you make first? And the answer comes straight from the information theory lesson. You want the question that reduces your uncertainty the most. So you compute the entropy of the labels before the split, then the weighted average entropy of the two groups after the split, and take the difference. That difference is called the information gain, and the tree simply tries every feature at every threshold and takes the split with the highest gain. Then it recurses. That is the entire training algorithm — no gradients, no learning rate, no iterations. Gini impurity is a common alternative to entropy here and behaves almost identically; do not spend time agonizing over which. Now, why practitioners genuinely love trees. They need no feature scaling at all, because a threshold does not care what units you measured in — this alone removes the most common source of bugs from the previous slide. They handle categorical features directly without one-hot encoding. They cope with missing values. They capture interactions automatically, because a split deeper in the tree is by construction conditional on the splits above it. And, most valuable of all in a real organization, the fitted model is a flowchart. You can print it, hand it to a doctor or a loan officer or a mechanic, and they can read it and tell you it is wrong — which is a form of validation no neural network offers. But a single tree has a fatal weakness, and it follows from the same recursion. Left to grow to full depth, the tree will keep splitting until every training row sits in its own leaf. Training accuracy hits one hundred percent, and the tree has memorized the noise exactly as Usopp memorized Syrup Village. So you must constrain it: limit the maximum depth, require a minimum number of samples in a leaf, or grow it fully and then prune branches back by validation performance.",
