      "heading": "Boosting, and why trees still win on tables",
      "narration": "One more ensemble, and then the practical conclusion that I most want you to carry into an interview. Random forests build their trees in parallel and independently. Boosting does the opposite: it builds them in sequence, where each new tree is fitted specifically to the errors the ensemble has made so far. Train a shallow tree, see where it is wrong, train the next shallow tree to predict those residuals, add it in with a small weight, repeat hundreds of times. The distinction is worth memorizing because it is a standard question: bagging, meaning random forests, reduces variance by averaging independent models. Boosting reduces bias by iteratively correcting a weak model. That makes boosting more powerful and correspondingly more delicate. Because each round deliberately chases the remaining errors, running too many rounds will eventually chase the noise, so boosting genuinely can overfit in a way that a random forest largely does not. It therefore needs a learning rate, needs early stopping, and needs a validation set watched carefully. A random forest is far more forgiving: throw more trees at it and it does not get worse, just slower. If you have one afternoon and no time to tune, use a forest. If you have a week and a leaderboard, use boosting. And now the conclusion. On tabular data — rows and columns, the kind of data that most companies actually have — gradient-boosted trees are still state of the art. XGBoost, LightGBM and CatBoost win competitions and production deployments today, in a world of trillion-parameter models. Deep learning has genuinely not displaced them here, and being able to say that calmly in an interview signals experience rather than fashion. The reason is worth understanding. Neural networks win where the data has structure they can exploit — spatial locality in images, sequence in text — and tables have neither. Table columns are heterogeneous and unordered; swapping two columns changes nothing about the meaning, which is exactly the invariance a convolution cannot use. Trees are naturally invariant to feature scale and naturally piecewise-constant, and real tabular relationships tend to be full of thresholds and interactions rather than smooth surfaces. Add that most tabular datasets are thousands of rows rather than millions, and the deep model never gets the data it needs. Use the tool that matches the shape of the problem.",
